      longDescription: "Payments Ledger is the core of a payments system: accounts, transfers and the guarantees that keep money from appearing, disappearing or moving twice. Every transaction is a set of entries that sum to zero per currency, posted atomically and exactly once per idempotency key.\n\nA transfer is one Postgres transaction. It first inserts the idempotency key, so a concurrent retry with the same key waits and then gets the original result back. It locks the accounts involved in id order, so two opposite transfers can never wait on each other. It checks currency and overdraft, which a database constraint also enforces, writes the entries, updates the balances and inserts an event into an outbox table. Relay workers claim outbox rows with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED and publish each one. It is Go 1.27 with the standard library router, pgx, errgroup and slog, and about 300 lines of hand-written SQL instead of an ORM.\n\nThe tests prove the guarantees against a real Postgres, with the race detector on: 50 simultaneous retries with one key produce exactly one transaction, 32 workers moving money in both directions never deadlock and never lose a cent, and no outbox event is published twice. CI fails if any of those tests is skipped. I built it in November 2026 to show correctness under concurrency, the part of backend work that a demo never shows.",
