      longDescription: "Vector Search in Go is the index behind semantic search, written from scratch to show where concurrency pays off and where it does not. It is HNSW, the graph that most vector databases use, in pure Go with nothing outside the standard library.\n\nThe build inserts vectors from several goroutines at once, with one mutex per node and an exclusive lock only when the top layer of the graph grows. Once the build ends, the index freezes: searches take no locks at all, and any later insert panics, so a reader can never meet a writer. Each search borrows its scratch space from a sync.Pool, and a small HTTP server answers POST /search behind a semaphore sized to the number of cores. Exact search sits next to it, as ground truth and as the baseline.\n\nOn BEIR SciFact, 5,183 abstracts embedded with bge-small at 384 dimensions, search throughput grew 8.75 times from 1 goroutine to 12, and 8 workers built the graph in 0.11 seconds instead of 0.79 with almost the same recall. Splitting one query across 8 graphs made it slower, so the index parallelizes between queries, not inside one. I built it in November 2026 as the Go concurrency counterpart to RAG Evals, which searches the same corpus with pgvector. The tests run with the race detector, including inserts and searches on the same index at the same time.",
